# The read under the key of a for-in uses the enumerator's index, after a check that may reuse the result of the enumerator's step.
# The read checks the object against the enumerator (first that it is a cell, then its structure and order word) with each of three switches, and with no other.
# The three are CheckBoundsAtRunTime (--aot-harden=ignore-told-ranges), CheckProofsAtRunTime (--aot-harden=proof-checks) and CheckBoundsInEmittersOnly.
# Fault injection, testing=AssumeEnumeratorCurrent: the check reuses the step's result although a call has deleted that very property.
# With the fault and any one of the switches, the process crashes with a named error. With any one of the switches alone, the result is right.
# RUN(RIGHTP): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# RIGHTP: ash1birch2cedar_date4elm5fir6gum7hazel8
# RIGHTP-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(RIGHTE): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckBoundsInEmittersOnly
# RIGHTE: ash1birch2cedar_date4elm5fir6gum7hazel8
# RIGHTE-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(HELDT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=AssumeEnumeratorCurrent,CheckBoundsAtRunTime
# HELDT: bun: internal AOT error:
# HELDT: crashed with SIGABRT
# RUN(HELDP): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=AssumeEnumeratorCurrent,CheckProofsAtRunTime
# HELDP: bun: internal AOT error:
# HELDP: crashed with SIGABRT
# RUN(HELDE): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=AssumeEnumeratorCurrent,CheckBoundsInEmittersOnly
# HELDE: bun: internal AOT error:
# HELDE: crashed with SIGABRT
